A rewind state leaves the next save's reservation alone

System::SaveStateRaw (0014) recorded its size in last_state_size, the figure
the next real save reserves its buffer with (SaveState, SaveStateBuffer, 0004).
A rewind state is taken without the emulated memories (0015): 12 to 16 MB,
where a save of a New 3DS game is about 296 MB. So after the first rewind
snapshot every save state, the auto-save on going to the background among
them, reserved 12 MB and grew its buffer as FCRAM was written, a doubling
holding the old block beside the new: the 768 MB peak 0004 removed, back
through the rewind, at the moment iOS gives the app the least time and memory.

SaveStateRaw no longer touches last_state_size; the raw state keeps its own
buffer between calls, which is all it needs.

--- a/src/core/savestate.cpp
+++ b/src/core/savestate.cpp
@@ -382,7 +382,8 @@
         oarchive oa{stream};
         oa&* this;
     }
-    last_state_size = sink.bytes.size();
+    // Not last_state_size: that is what the next real save reserves, and a
+    // raw rewind state (without the memories, 0015) is a twentieth of one.
     sink.bytes.swap(buffer);
 }
 
